{"causes":["Gibran's experiences as an immigrant in the United States shaped his unique perspective on the human condition, which is reflected in 'The Prophet'.","His exploration of Eastern mysticism influenced his writing style and themes, drawing upon elements of Sufism and other spiritual traditions.","The cultural and artistic movements of the time, including Expressionism and the Harlem Renaissance, provided a fertile ground for Gibran's innovative work."],"effects":["Gibran's poetry has had a profound impact on modern literature, influencing generations of writers and poets.","His emphasis on individualism and spirituality has resonated with readers around the world, making 'The Prophet' a beloved classic of 20th-century literature.","The book's exploration of universal themes such as love, freedom, and the human condition continues to inspire artists, musicians, and filmmakers today."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Alfred_A._Knopf","sourceName":"Alfred A. Knopf","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The_Prophet","targetName":"Kahlil Gibran's 'The Prophet'","context":"First published in 1923 by Alfred A. Knopf."},{"sourceSlug":"Harlem_Renaissance","sourceName":"Harlem Renaissance","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The_Prophet","targetName":"Kahlil Gibran's 'The Prophet'","context":"Cultural and artistic movements of the time influenced Gibran's innovative work."},{"sourceSlug":"Expressionism","sourceName":"Expressionism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The_Prophet","targetName":"Kahlil Gibran's 'The Prophet'","context":"Artistic movements of the time, including Expressionism, provided a fertile ground for Gibran's work."},{"sourceSlug":"Sufism","sourceName":"Sufism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The_Prophet","targetName":"Kahlil Gibran's 'The Prophet'","context":"Gibran's exploration of Eastern mysticism drew upon elements of Sufism."},{"sourceSlug":"Lebanon","sourceName":"Lebanon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The_Prophet","targetName":"Kahlil Gibran's 'The Prophet'","context":"Gibran's experiences as a Lebanese immigrant in the United States shaped his unique perspective on the human condition."}],"places":[{"name":"New York City, United States","role":"Home"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Exile"},{"name":"Beirut, Lebanon","role":"Birthplace"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Prophet","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-prophet"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1196302","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Prophet_(book)"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Prophet contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Prophet is a collection of 26 poetic essays (or prose poems) by the Lebanese-American poet, artist, and philosopher Kahlil Gibran (1883–1931), first published in English by Alfred A. Knopf in New York on 23 September 1923. The book presents the wisdom teachings of a prophetic figure named Almustafa ('the chosen and the beloved'), who, on the day of his departure from the city of Orphalese after twelve years, is asked by the people to speak on the great themes of human life — Love, Marriage, Children, Work, Joy and Sorrow, Houses, Clothes, Buying and Selling, Crime and Punishment, Laws, Freedom, Reason and Passion, Pain, Self-Knowledge, Teaching, Friendship, Talking, Time, Good and Evil, Prayer, Pleasure, Beauty, Religion, and Death.

The Prophet is the best-selling book ever published by Alfred A. Knopf and one of the best-selling books of the 20th century — it remained in print continuously from its first publication, was translated into over 100 languages, and sold over 100 million copies worldwide by the end of the 20th century. It achieved its greatest cultural impact during the counter-culture movement of the 1960s–1970s, when its themes of spiritual freedom, universal love, and transcendence of social convention resonated with the counter-culture's rejection of conventional religion and politics. Passages from The Prophet are regularly read at weddings, funerals, and religious ceremonies, and its aphoristic style has made it one of the most widely quoted books of the 20th century.

Gibran's prose style — biblical in its rhythms, Sufi in its imagery, and Transcendentalist in its philosophical temper — synthesises Arabic literary tradition (Arabic poetry, Islamic mysticism), Blake's prophetic books, Nietzsche's Zarathustra, and American Transcendentalism (Emerson, Whitman) into a distinctively accessible mystical idiom. The Prophet is the most commercially successful work of Arabic-language literature in translation and established Gibran as the third best-selling poet in history (after Shakespeare and Laozi).","subjects":["Lebanese Literature","Modern Era","Kahlil Gibran","Prose Poetry","Spiritual Literature","Arab-American Literature","Counter-Culture","20th Century"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Kahlil Gibran's 'The Prophet' has had a profound impact on modern literature and culture, shaping the way we think about spirituality, individualism, and the human condition.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
